  "query_preview": "..\n\nBroad surface high spread across most of the Great Lakes, Ontario \nand Quebec will keep a cool easterly flow across the region through \nthe bulk of the week. Mid level split flow pattern will help keep \nthe current conditions stagnate through the week as well. 850mb \ntemps will hover in the mid-upper single digits setting the stage \nfor highs in the 60s all week with low dipping into the 40s. The \ncool air flowing over the relatively warm waters will open the door \nfor some cloud cover which would largely affect locations west of \nLake Erie. With clearer skies across Mid MI, could see a gradient in \ntemps from the low 60s across the south under the blanket of clouds \nand upper 60s northward with the additional sun. \n\nA mid level low will ride along the southern stream of the split \nflow through Ohio Tuesday night into Wednesday. This will bring \ndeeper moisture depths back north into southern MI with model \nsoundings showing saturation layers from 2-10kft with some mid level \ndeformation just north of the low with low level convergence south \nof I94 as well. This is the only period in the week which will \nsupport a chance of precip as once the low passes Wednesday, heights \nbegin to rise and surface high pressure settle back southward. \n\nWeak troughing over the east coast will strengthen mid week in part \nbecause a mid level wave tracking across northern Ohio phases with \nit. Moreso the deepening will occur because the overall longwave \npattern amplifies in response to the next trough coming ashore the \nPacific NW later in the week. This causes the Plains ridge to \namplify and shift over the Great Lakes and stalls the downstream \nupper low over the East Coast. This will all result in a gradual \nwarm up across the region this weekend back into the low 70s with \ndry conditions continuing.",
